UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 10, Lesson 1: Rocks and soils', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Rocks and soils have observable properties (hardness, grain size, colour, how they hold water) that help us describe and compare samples.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nCompare sand and clay: two property differences and one way each affects plant watering.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSand drains fast / gritty; clay holds water / sticky — plants in sand may need more frequent water; clay can stay wetter.\n\nWATCH FOR\nCalling all brown ground “dirt” without properties skips scientific description.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nTest four soil/rock samples (touch, grain, water soak if allowed). Complete a property table and sort samples with a written rule.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science and Technology","learning_intention":"Rocks and soils have observable properties (hardness, grain size, colour, how they hold water) that help us describe and compare samples.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["ST2-SCI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Test four soil/rock samples (touch, grain, water soak if allowed). Complete a property table and sort samples with a written rule. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about rock linked to that evidence.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Calling all brown ground “dirt” without properties skips scientific description.. Correction: Sand drains fast / gritty; with evidence.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Calling all brown ground “dirt” without properties skips scientific description. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses rock. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about rock, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about rock. Reject title-only labels with no observation. / Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for rock. Reject wording changes that leave the same error..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for rock. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Sand drains fast / gritty","Calling all brown ground “dirt” without properties skips scientific description"],"question":"Compare sand and clay: two property differences and one way each affects plant watering. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for rock; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Calling all brown ground “dirt” without properties skips scientific description","Rocks and soils have observable properties (hardness, grain size, colour, how they hold water) that help us describe","Sand drains fast / gritty","Careful use of soil and explain why it matters with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with rock?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named soil and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Calling all brown ground “dirt” without properties skips scientific description","Name soil and explain why it matters only, without linking it to rock","Apply rock to new material and name the soil and explain why it matters used"],"question":"Which response best applies rock with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Calling all brown ground “dirt” without properties skips scientific description","Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone","Decide from which object looks most exciting","Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation"],"question":"New examples: yeast making bread rise, a spinning fan, copper wire, a tadpole. Which claim about rock is most trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Calling all brown ground “dirt” without properties skips scientific description","A named observation plus how it supports the claim","A longer story with no observation","A label copied from the title with no evidence"],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about rock?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The strongest choice applies the taught idea about rock with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","Sand drains fast / gritty","Calling all brown ground “dirt” without properties skips scientific description","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster"],"question":"Using this science idea — Rocks and soils have observable properties (hardness, grain size, colour, how they hold water) that — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable, control the rest, and use recorded evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Stop early when you like the first result","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","Change one variable and keep the other conditions the same","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster"],"question":"Which investigation design best tests an idea about rock?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Scientific predictions stay open to new observations rather than protecting a first guess. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Calling all brown ground “dirt” without properties skips scientific description","Results that disagree with the first guess should be discarded","If the observed feature changes, the claim about rock should change with it","Any exciting story about the object must be true"],"question":"Which prediction about rock can be checked with evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about rock. Reject title-only labels with no observation.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses rock. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about rock linked to that evidence.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for rock. Reject wording changes that leave the same error.","question":"Name one science misconception about rock, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Calling all brown ground “dirt” without properties skips scientific description.. Correction: Sand drains fast / gritty; with evidence.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y4-w10-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise rock.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Earth and Space","success_criteria":["I can use rock with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify soil and explain why it matters.","I can practise property independently on a new prompt.","I can notice compare needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Rocks and soils have observable properties (hardness, grain size, colour, how they hold water) that help us describe and compare samples.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Rocks and soils have observable properties (hardness, grain size, colour, how they hold water) that help us describe"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about rock with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSand soil feels gritty and drains quickly; clay feels sticky when wet and holds water. A rock may be hard and crystalline or layered like sandstone.\n\nTRY FIRST\nCompare sand and clay: two property differences and one way each affects plant watering.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSand drains fast / gritty; clay holds water / sticky — plants in sand may need more frequent water; clay can stay wetter.","explain":"Rocks and soils have observable properties (hardness, grain size, colour, how they hold water) that help us describe and compare samples.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Calling all brown ground “dirt” without properties skips scientific description. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Soil properties.","caption":"Describe, do not only say dirt.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Sand</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">gritty drains</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Clay</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">sticky holds water</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Sand soil feels gritty and drains quickly; clay feels sticky when wet and holds water. A rock may be hard and crystallin Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Sand soil feels gritty and drains quickly; clay feels sticky when wet and holds water. A rock may be hard and crystallin Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about rock with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSand soil feels gritty and drains quickly; clay feels sticky when wet and holds water. A rock may be hard and crystalline or layered like sandstone. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show science thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Compare samples.","caption":"Four-sample table.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Rock</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">hard / layered</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Sort rule</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">from properties</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about rock with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCalling all brown ground “dirt” without properties skips scientific description. Here is a more productive model: Sand soil feels gritty and drains quickly; clay feels sticky when wet and holds water. A rock may be hard and crystalline or layered like sandstone.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSand drains fast / gritty; clay holds water / sticky — plants in sand may need more frequent water; clay can stay wetter.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Calling all brown ground “dirt” without properties skips scientific description. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about rock with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRocks and soils have observable properties (hardness, grain size, colour, how they hold water) that help us describe and compare samples. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 10, Lesson 1: Rocks and soils","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Earth surface and weather","week":10,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nSand soil feels gritty and drains quickly; clay feels sticky when wet and holds water. A rock may be hard and crystalline or layered like sandstone.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Soil properties.","caption":"Describe, do not only say dirt.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" 